course: everyday
lesson: 269
title: Crash forces, school buses and public buses
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: In a crash the forces are so strong that no arms can hold a child, which is why every child needs their own belt or seat.
explain: A bus is big and tall, so its driver cannot see a child who bends down close to it, and the bus hides children from cars and cars from children.
explain: That is why you never pick up something you dropped near the bus — you tell the driver first — and you cross only when the bus has left and you can see the road clearly.
explain: On a public bus you have the fare or card ready, you press the button or pull the cord before your stop, and you share the small space kindly.
explain: A girl in London falls asleep on the bus and wakes up one stop too late; she gets off at the next stop, phones her mum and walks back — nothing to be ashamed of.
fact: In a crash nobody can hold a child with their arms, because the forces are far stronger than any arms.
fact: If you drop something near the school bus, you never bend down to pick it up; you tell the driver first, because the driver may not see you.
fact: Bullying on the bus is told to the driver, a teacher or a parent, because adults should stop it.
fact: When you get off, you make sure bag straps, drawstrings and loose clothes do not catch on the handrail or door.
fact: You have the fare, a travel card or a contactless card ready before getting on, to keep the line moving.
fact: You press the stop button or pull the cord before your stop, because the driver will not stop unless someone asks.
fact: Smelly food is eaten after you get off, because others share the small space.
fact: After getting off you wait until the bus has left and you can see the road clearly before crossing.
fact: If you miss your stop, you get off at the next one, tell the driver if needed and phone a parent.
fact: On the top deck of a double-decker you stay seated and hold the rail on the stairs, because the stairs move with the bus.
exercise: You drop your glove next to the school bus. What do you do? | Tell the driver first and never bend down near the bus | Quickly bend down and grab it | Crawl under the bus to reach it | Ask a friend to hold the door
exercise: Why is it dangerous to bend down near a school bus? | The driver may not see you and may start moving | Buses are dirty and oily underneath | The bus might honk at you | Your bag might fall open and spill everything out onto the road
exercise: An older boy keeps taking your hat on the school bus. What do you do? | Tell the driver, a teacher or a parent | Keep quiet and hope it stops | Take his bag in return | Stop riding the bus without telling anyone
exercise: What should you check as you get off the bus? | That bag straps will not catch on the rail or door | That your shoelaces are double knotted | That your seat is clean and nothing is left behind on it for the next child | That the windows are closed
exercise: Why can loose straps be dangerous when getting off a bus? | They can catch on the door and drag a child | They make the bus slow down at every stop | They make it hard to find your seat | They are against school uniform rules
exercise: What do you do before getting on a public bus? | Have your fare or travel card ready | Look for your money after you get on | Ask the driver to wait while you search | Get on first and pay at the end of the trip
exercise: How does the bus driver know you want to get off? | You press the stop button or pull the cord before your stop | The driver stops at every stop anyway | You stand up when the bus passes your stop | You wave at the driver's mirror so he can see you in it as he drives
exercise: You have a bag of hot chips on the bus. What is polite? | Wait until you get off to eat them | Eat them quickly before anyone notices | Share them with the person next to you | Open the window and eat them there quickly
exercise: You get off the bus and need to cross the road behind it. What do you do? | Wait until the bus has left and you can see clearly | Cross right behind the bus straight away | Cross in front of the bus while it waits, as the driver can see you there | Run across before the bus pulls away
exercise: Why should you not cross right behind a bus that is still standing? | The bus hides you from cars and cars from you | Buses often reverse at every stop | The driver will be cross with you for crossing behind his bus | The bus fumes are too hot
exercise: You fall asleep and miss your stop. What do you do? | Get off at the next stop and phone a parent | Stay on to the end of the line and wait | Jump off while the bus is moving | Hide so nobody sees
exercise: You are on the top deck of a London double-decker. What do you do? | Stay seated and hold the rail on the stairs | Stand at the front window to see the road better | Run down the stairs when your stop comes into view | Walk around while the bus moves, holding the seat backs as you go
exercise: Why should you hold the rail on a double-decker's stairs? | The stairs move with the bus | The stairs are made of slippery glass | The rail is warm to hold in winter | The driver will not go until you do
exercise: A mum says she can hold her son on her lap in the car. Which is true? | In a crash nobody can hold a child with their arms | A mum's arms are stronger than a seat belt | Children are safe on a lap on short trips near home | A crash only pushes things gently
test: A boy in Ohio sees his ball roll under the school bus. What is the safest choice? | Stay back and tell the driver at once | Reach under quickly before the bus moves | Ask a friend to watch the driver while he reaches | Wait for the bus to leave and then chase the ball
test: A girl in Auckland reaches her stop but nobody presses the button. What is likely? | The bus will drive on past her stop | The driver will stop anyway because he knows she lives there | The bus will stop at every stop on the way | The bus will turn back round for her
test: Why do people have their fare ready before getting on? | It keeps the line moving quickly | Drivers do not accept money | It makes the bus go faster | The ticket is cheaper that way
test: A boy steps off the bus, and his long scarf is still caught in the closing door. What should he have done? | Kept his scarf tucked in so it could not catch | Jumped off faster | Held on to the door with both hands so it could not close on him | Pulled the scarf after the bus moved
test: A child is being called names on the school bus every day. Who can help? | The driver, a teacher or a parent | Nobody, because it happens on the bus | Only the other children | The bus company website
test: Which is true about crossing near a bus that has just stopped? | It is safest to wait until the bus has gone | It is safest to cross in front of the bus | Cars always stop behind buses and wait | Buses keep the road clear for you, so cars cannot come past them
test: A girl in Belfast realises she is two stops past home. What is sensible? | Get off at the next stop and phone a parent | Cry and stay on the bus | Ask a stranger to drive her home | Get off and walk on the motorway
test: A boy stands on the top deck to look out of the front window as the bus turns. What could happen? | He could fall, because the bus sways when it turns | Nothing, because buses move smoothly round every corner | The bus will stop because he is standing | The window will open
test: Someone opens a strong-smelling lunch on a crowded bus. Why is that unkind? | Everyone shares the small space and the smell | Food is not allowed in any vehicle anywhere | Buses have their own lunch time | The driver must eat first, before any of the passengers are allowed to eat
test: dropped my pencil case by the school bus wheel | Tell the driver first, never just bend down for it | Grab it quickly before the bus moves off | Crawl under to get it | Kick it out with your foot
test: we got off the bus, wanna cross in front of it | Wait till the bus has gone and you can see the road | Cross in front right away | Run behind the bus | Cross while it's moving off, the driver will wait for you
test: missed my stop!! | Get off at the next one and phone a parent | Jump off while it moves | Stay on till the end of the line, then walk all the way back alone | Shout at the driver
test: upstairs on the double decker, can we stand at the front and surf | No, stay seated, and hold the rail on the stairs | Yes, it's like a ride | Yes, if you hold the window frame with both hands | Only when it goes fast
explain: A girl in Texas bent down to pick up a pencil by the front wheel of her school bus; the driver could not see her, the bus moved off and she was badly hurt.
explain: A boy in Leeds ran across the road from behind his bus to catch up with a friend; a van passing the bus could not see him in time and hit him, and he spent weeks in hospital with a broken leg.
fact: Most children hurt around school buses are hit outside the bus, near its front or sides, not inside it.
fact: A child stepping out from behind a stopped bus is hidden from drivers until the very last moment, so a driver has no time to stop.
test: my mate's bag strap got stuck in the bus door and it started going, wat now | Shout to the driver to stop and get an adult at once | Grab the strap and pull hard yourself | Run alongside the bus holding the bag | Wait and see if it comes loose by itself
test: lily says its fine to cross right in front of the bus, driver can see u. true? | No, the driver may not see a child close to the front | Yes, drivers always see the whole road | Yes, buses always wait until every single kid has crossed | Yes, if you run it is fine
test: why do kids get hurt near school buses not on them mostly | They step into blind spots or out from behind the bus | The buses are older than cars | Bus seats have no belts | Buses drive faster than cars do, even near schools and in towns
test: did u hear?? a kid at our stop bent down for his phone by the bus wheel and the driver never saw him, he's in hospital now. what's the lesson | Never bend down near a bus; tell the driver first | Buses are dangerous so never ride them | Phones should never come to school in the first place | Only little kids get hurt near buses
test: my cousin says a girl got dragged half a mile cos her scarf caught in the bus door, she swears it's true. what's likely | The door catch is a real danger but the distance is probably blown up | Every word of it must be exactly true | Scarves can never catch in bus doors | Bus doors cannot close on anything at all, they have sensors that always stop them
test: everyone's saying the boy who ran out behind the bus was "being an idiot, serves him right". pass it on? | No, don't spread it; just learn to wait till the bus has gone | Yes, so everyone knows he was silly | Yes, and add what you heard about him | Yes, but only to the kids in his class, and to his friends and their brothers
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the conduct required of a pupil whose property has fallen adjacent to a stationary school bus? | The pupil shall notify the driver and refrain from stooping | The pupil shall retrieve the item with all possible haste | The pupil shall crawl beneath the vehicle to recover it | The pupil shall request another pupil to obstruct the doors meanwhile
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a pedestrian alighting from a bus is required to defer crossing until the vehicle has departed. | The vehicle conceals the pedestrian from traffic and vice versa | Buses customarily reverse upon leaving each stop | The driver is obliged to supervise every crossing | Exhaust fumes render the area behind a bus impassable
exam: It is given that a passenger upon a public omnibus wishes to alight at a particular stop. Select the response consistent with the established procedure. | The passenger shall press the button or pull the cord before the stop | The driver shall halt at every stop irrespective of request | The passenger shall stand once the stop has been passed | The passenger shall signal to the driver by means of the interior mirror
exam: Whereas it is sometimes asserted that an adult may secure a child upon the lap during a motor journey, state which proposition is correct. | In a collision the forces exceed what any adult's arms can restrain | An adult's arms afford protection superior to a seat belt | A lap is adequate restraint upon short journeys at modest speed | Collisions exert only modest forces upon passengers
